    "titleMy": "ビジネス電話と「ウチ・ソト」の敬語ルール (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "titleMyanmar": "ビジネス電話と「ウチ・ソト」の敬語ルール (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "translationMy": "In Japanese business etiquette, the easiest mistake to make is using keigo during telephone correspondence. In Japanese culture, there is a clear distinction between \"Uchi\" (one's own family or company members) and \"Soto\" (outsiders and customers).\n\nFor example, suppose a client calls and asks: \"Is Director Sato there?\" At this time, even though Sato is your superior within your company, when speaking to someone outside the company, you must treat him as an \"in-group person\" and use humble speech (kenjougo).\n\nTherefore, responding with respectful speech: \"Sato-bucho is not here (irasshaimasen)\" is a mistake. The correct response is to omit the title suffix \"bucho\" to refer to him simply as Sato, and answer: \"I am terribly sorry. Our director, Sato, has currently stepped away from his desk (seki o hazushite orimasu).\"\n\nIn this manner, lowering not only your own actions but also the actions of your in-group members depending on who the listener is represents the fundamental characteristic of humble keigo. Acquiring this distinction between inside and outside is the first step toward becoming a trusted professional."
