    "titleMy": "日本語の会話を滑らかにするつなぎ言葉 (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "titleMyanmar": "日本語の会話を滑らかにするつなぎ言葉 (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "translationMy": "When you first begin studying Japanese, you tend to be fully occupied just constructing words and grammar accurately one by one. However, in order to communicate naturally with Japanese people in daily conversation, skillfully mastering connecting expressions (discourse markers) that link sentences is essential.\n\nFor example, when you want to switch to a new topic after listening thoroughly to the other person, the expression \"tokorode\" (by the way) is extremely useful. By using it like \"Tokorode, do you have any plans for next week's holidays?\", you can transition topics naturally without giving an abrupt impression.\n\nAlso, when conveying your opinion along with reasons, adding reasons using \"sore ni\" (moreover) greatly increases persuasiveness. Saying \"The food at this shop is delicious. Moreover, the staff's customer service is kind,\" makes it easy for the other person to be convinced.\n\nFurthermore, when broaching your true thoughts in a conversation, placing the preface \"Jitsu wa...\" (Actually...) makes the listener pay close attention, realizing \"There must be an important matter.\" After all, learning discourse markers that act as conversational lubricant is the best shortcut to natural Japanese."
